WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.919 --> 00:00:07.470
Black Magic - this is the name of Loyiso Mkize's fifth solo exhibition, which he is currently

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:09.080
preparing for.

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:11.840
But his art has nothing to do with evil spirits.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:17.140
For Loyiso Mkize, it's about the black experience transforming into something beautiful and

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:18.300
powerful.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:24.779
I believe that we as a people, particularly
in Africa, are going through a really exciting

00:00:24.779 --> 00:00:27.519
transition right now in history.

00:00:27.519 --> 00:00:34.609
We come from a very horrific history and there is a sense of self now that is very different

00:00:34.609 --> 00:00:35.850
and very urgent.

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:44.710
Cause I believe our story is yet to be told
in its totality.

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:50.909
Social media played a defining role in Mkize’s career: After his work was discovered online

00:00:50.909 --> 00:00:56.729
in 2014, he was commissioned to do nine pieces for Momentum, an action film starring Morgan

00:00:56.729 --> 00:00:58.510
Freeman.

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:02.559
Soon Mkize’s work caught the interest of
galleries and art dealers.

00:01:02.559 --> 00:01:07.290
His powerful portraits reveal the dignity
of his subjects, while addressing major social

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:08.290
issues.

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:12.829
Tonight Mkize will celebrate the opening of Black Magic in a Cape Town gallery.

00:01:12.829 --> 00:01:20.420
It is relevant because what I see what kind of topics he explores is like you know the

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:25.289
black identity is a lot present in his work
and how he is trying to promote you know the

00:01:25.289 --> 00:01:29.820
fact that you should be proud of who you are and he is really proud of his own background

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:33.759
and he is trying to bring that across in his
work.

00:01:33.759 --> 00:01:40.971
Mkize explores themes of power, African heritage, and modernity, not only as a painter, but

00:01:40.971 --> 00:01:45.240
also as a comic book artist.

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:51.369
He is the creator of Kwezi - a superhero who lives in a squatter camp.

00:01:51.369 --> 00:01:56.740
Kwezi has to learn to embrace his African
identity first, before he can use his powers

00:01:56.740 --> 00:02:00.670
to fight evil and corruption.

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:05.939
Kwezi is popular with young South African’s - such as Kirsten Muller, who we meet in a

00:02:05.939 --> 00:02:07.049
popular bar.

00:02:07.049 --> 00:02:11.009
A Kwezi fan, she immediately recognises Mkize and joins us at our table.

00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:18.410
I really just like the fact that South Africa
now has like an actually comic book that we

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:23.400
can call our own you know and our characters we can relate to them.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:29.060
Mkize sees many hidden super-human qualities
in the younger generation of South Africans.

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:34.261
With his African heroes, Mkize wants to change young people’s frame of reference and create

00:02:34.261 --> 00:02:40.960
positive imagery that helps them to see their history and identity in an empowering light.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:46.599
What we know about super heroes, we know that they embody our greatest ambition as human

00:02:46.599 --> 00:02:47.599
beings.

00:02:47.599 --> 00:02:53.010
And that I think says a lot about the potential number one of the people that we take for

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:54.379
granted in South Africa.

00:02:54.379 --> 00:03:01.120
The people that come from the township that come from the villages you know and that’s

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:08.469
what super hero comic books are about.

